WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.581
Backoff with jitter, and why the jitter matters.

2
00:00:02.861 --> 00:00:10.093
The standard retry schedule is exponential backoff: wait a base delay, then double it on each attempt, up to a cap.

3
00:00:10.373 --> 00:00:12.485
If attempt 0 waits up to 0.

4
00:00:12.765 --> 00:00:15.432
5 seconds, attempt 3 waits up to 4.

5
00:00:15.712 --> 00:00:18.827
The doubling gives an overloaded service room to recover.

6
00:00:19.107 --> 00:00:32.568
Backoff alone has a flaw that Marc Brooker described on the AWS Architecture Blog: if many clients fail at the same moment, they all retry on the same schedule and arrive together again, in synchronized waves.

7
00:00:32.848 --> 00:00:35.493
Adding randomness, called jitter, spreads them out.

8
00:00:35.773 --> 00:00:47.763
His “full jitter” variant picks the delay uniformly between zero and the exponential value, and his simulations showed it reduced both total work and completion time compared with no jitter.

9
00:00:48.043 --> 00:00:56.363
In symbols: delay for attempt n is a uniform random number in the interval from 0 to min(cap, base times 2 to the n).

10
00:00:56.643 --> 00:00:59.800
If the server sent retry-after, wait at least that long.
